@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.Head
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.LearnPage
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.SiteUrl
@param page: LearnPage
@param head: Head
@param site: SiteUrl

<%--
    /learn/reversible-pcb. NO BACKTICKS anywhere in this file -- one closes the raw string
    this page is written in, and jte then reports the error on the wrong line.

    THE EXAMPLE BOARDS, AND HOW EACH WAS CHECKED (2026-10-03). The corpus records only
    whether a split is reversible, never how, so every method named below rests on the
    board's own file or a build photo, and a board nobody checked is not named:
      - Architeuthis Dux: seed/bodies.toml, owner-confirmed 2026-10-02. One board file of
        17 key positions for a 34-key design, a plain 24-hole Pro Micro footprint, and a
        build photo with one nice!nano face up and the other face down.
      - Cradio Z lp: davidphilipbarr/36keys, 34Keys/Cradio-Z lp/cradio-z.kicad_pcb. One
        board file, 17 switches, and a ProMicro_v2 footprint of 50 pads: two overlapping
        24-hole sets, mirror images of each other. Its body.reversible is still unrecorded
        in the corpus, which is the owner's to fill (seed/bodies.toml).
      - Ferris: seed/bodies.toml, owner's ruling 2026-10-01: the left half places the chip
        and the USB-C receptacle, the right half the MCP23017 IO expander.

    The diagram is a sketch, NOT to scale: it shows which face a part is on, nothing else.
--%>
@template.layout(head = head, site = site, content = @`
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.articleLd}</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.breadcrumbLd}</script>
    <article class="learn">
        <p class="crumb"><a href="/learn">&larr; Learn</a></p>
        <h1>Reversible PCBs</h1>

        <p class="learn-answer">
            A reversible PCB is one board design that makes both halves of a split
            keyboard. Build the left half on one board, flip the next one over and build
            the right half on that. You order one design instead of two.
        </p>

        <figure class="learn-figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 152 52" role="img"
                 aria-label="Three sketches of one keyboard half. First, the left half with its controller on top. Second, the same board flipped over to make the right half: the controller's holes now face the other way, so the controller goes on the other face. Third, a board with two mirrored sets of controller holes, so the controller can sit on top on both halves.">
                <rect class="lf-board" x="4" y="12" width="40" height="26" rx="2"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="7" y="15" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="7" y="25" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="15" y="15" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="15" y="25" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="23" y="15" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="23" y="25" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="33" y="15" width="8" height="20" rx="0.6"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="17" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="19.6" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="22.2" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="24.8" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="27.4" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="30" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="34.2" cy="32.6" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="17" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="19.6" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="22.2" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="24.8" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="27.4" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="30" r="0.45"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="39.8" cy="32.6" r="0.45"/>
                <rect class="lf-board" x="56" y="12" width="40" height="26" rx="2"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="86" y="15" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="86" y="25" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="78" y="15" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
